<SddRoot keywords="root, portal, vision, scope-graph, idempotent, project-setup, infra-bootstrap" type="directive" ver="2.0">
  <Mission>
    Owner of `specs/README.md` — the Project Portal: Vision + Scope Graph + Scopes table.
    Idempotent: runs any number of times to refine vision, extend the graph, or bootstrap the
    first infra-base spec. Loaded lazily by the router; the router-core beliefs it relies on are
    declared in `BeliefState deps` (inherited, not repeated).

    Input — operator intent at project level. Output:
    - `specs/README.md` with Vision + Scope Graph + Scopes table.
    - Optional minimal `specs/infra-base/infra-base.spec.md` when the operator declared tooling.

    When the portal is absent, root reads the probe facts already in the `sdd-state` snapshot: a greenfield repo (no
    code) is initialized here; a repo that already holds code is handed to the from-code recovery branch
    (`discover-from-code`) — root never invents a vision over code that already exists.

    Out of scope: design of any individual scope (its vision, architecture, requirements,
    modules) — that is the scope branch. Root writes only the project-level map.
  </Mission>

  <BeliefState deps="AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE, AX_TOOL_INVOCATION, AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE, AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE, AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD, AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION, AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT, AX_READER_WITHOUT_SESSION_CONTEXT">
    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-portal-primary-owner"}}

    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-idempotent-modes"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-scope-graph-discipline"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-separation-of-concerns"}}

    {{> "axiom/infra/ax-infra-base-bootstrap"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-scope-table-format"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-vision-only-for-application"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-decision-log-required"}}

    {{> "axiom/process/ax-close-with-integrity-check"}}
  </BeliefState>

  <ChatProtocol>
    {{> "contract/process/question-format"}}
  </ChatProtocol>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_INTENT` | Operator invoked the root flow with empty intake against an existing Portal |
    | `H_CYCLE_IN_GRAPH` | Scope Graph contains a cycle (detected by `sdd-check`) |
    | `H_SCOPE_VS_MODULE_AMBIGUITY` | Operator labels as «scope» something that resembles a module |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected Vision, Scope Graph, or the final Portal |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Determine today's change set from state.</Goal>
      <Action>
        State from the router (`sdd-state`) already reports whether `specs/README.md` exists.
        - Absent → no portal yet: read `CODE` from the `sdd-state` output already in context — the
          probe (`CODE`/`INFRA`, code-dirs, configs) is part of the default snapshot; do NOT call
          `sdd-state` a second time for it.
          - `CODE=absent` (empty / only package.json) → **greenfield init**. The operator already saw the
            `sdd-state` card, shown verbatim as its own fenced unit by the router's SHOW-gate, and, for
            empty intake, confirmed «Создать проект» at the router's entry Ask (`AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE`)
            — the interview opens HERE, never before that card and never as free text dumped into the reply.
            An interview question that precedes the card, or that re-summarizes it instead of the router
            having shown it, violates the same gate the router enforces (`H_ASK_WITHOUT_CARD`) — a
            summary of the card is not the card. The project name is NOT asked here: default it silently
            to the repository folder's own name, kebab-case as-is, no invented embellishment — it then
            appears in every later show, and the operator may correct it inline in any free-text answer
            to STEP_1 or STEP_2 without a dedicated name question ever existing. One decision per question
            (`AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD`): naming is resolved silently here precisely so STEP_1 can ask vision
            alone and STEP_2 can ask scopes alone — never bundle name + vision + scopes into one opening
            question again.
          - `CODE=present` → **from-code recovery** — do NOT invent a vision over existing code:
            `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/discover-from-code.directive.xml")`.
        - Present → empty intake → `H_NO_INTENT`; otherwise classify the change:
          vision / add scope / update graph / infra bootstrap. Root has no hard modes — create when
          absent, propose intake-derived updates when present, per `AX_IDEMPOTENT_MODES`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_VISION">
      <Goal>Lock the project-level Vision, routing every anchor of the operator's statement to the
      artifact that owns it.</Goal>
      <Action>
        No product / library scope → skip Vision (`AX_VISION_ONLY_FOR_APPLICATION`). Else, on greenfield
        init (no Vision recorded yet), ask ONE open question through `AskUserQuestion` when available,
        otherwise `QUESTION_FORMAT`: «Что строим? Опиши замысел свободно.» with an escape option «Пока не
        знаю — предложи сам». Vision is the one field of genuine human intent in this step — the agent
        never fabricates it unasked, because a wrong guess here is an expensive semantic error to unwind
        later.

        Once the operator answers, the agent does NOT compress the answer — it NORMALIZES it while
        ROUTING every semantic anchor to the artifact it actually belongs to; dropping an anchor is a
        loss, but filing it under the wrong artifact is a second, independent loss (a release boundary
        misread as the target state, or a target state watered down by a caveat that only applies to v1).
        Three anchor kinds, three homes:
        - **Target state** — what the finished thing does, for whom, the full shape of the outcome →
          Vision, worded as the COMPLETE goal with no release caveat folded in. «Синхронизация между
          устройствами» stays the target even when v1 ships without it.
        - **Deferred / release-boundary decision** — «в v1 без бэкенда», «пока без синхронизации», «не
          сейчас» → a `AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED` entry in `DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`, marked deferred.
          Example: operator says «v1 строго local-only, без бэкенда» while the goal is full sync → Vision
          keeps «полная синхронизация между устройствами» as the target, and the Decision Log gets
          `D-001 <date> — v1 без бэкенда (почему: быстрее показать локальный сценарий; отложено до: этап
          синхронизации; риск: придётся менять модель хранения данных)`.
        - **Technical/stack preference** — a named framework, «готовый UIKit», a design-system pick →
          carried forward as intake for the step that actually owns implementation choices (STEP_2's
          scope-boundary call, STEP_3's tool-stack lock, or the later `scope`/`infra` session) — it never
          becomes a Vision sentence; Vision describes outcome, not toolchain.

        Every anchor the operator actually said lands in exactly one of the three homes above — the
        reader test from `AX_READER_WITHOUT_SESSION_CONTEXT` (would a specialist with zero memory of this
        session understand and act on it?) applies to each home's own wording.

        With anchors routed, close the remaining gaps in the Vision's own facets — for whom, the pain,
        the success criterion, the target state itself, guiding principles — through a microinterview
        that asks ONLY about facets the operator's words left open (`AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH`): a
        facet already covered by a routed anchor is never asked about again. Bundle every open facet into
        ONE `AskUserQuestion` call as independent questions — options drawn from real product practice
        plus the tool's own free-text slot for a custom answer, `multiSelect` where a facet genuinely
        allows more than one true answer. A rich statement closes most facets on its own, leaving 0-1
        follow-up questions; a thin one-liner leaves several open, and the full round runs.

        Assemble the structured Vision block from the routed target-state anchor plus the microinterview
        answers: one paragraph carrying the target state (no release caveats), followed by short
        strong-labelled sub-bullets — **Для кого**, **Боль**, **Успех**, **Принципы** — each filled from
        what the operator actually said, never fabricated for an unasked facet. Russian text quality
        follows `AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE` (flat, plain, no calque); the same bar audit checks later under
        `AX_LANGUAGE_QUALITY_CHECK` applies here at authoring time, not just at review time. The assembled
        block, and any Decision Log entries routed alongside it, are proposed back for confirm-or-edit —
        never treated as locked without that confirmation. If the operator picks the escape option, draft
        a Vision candidate from whatever context already exists (project name, intake, README) and
        present it back the same way. On an idempotent update (a Vision already exists), propose an
        intake-derived revision instead of asking from scratch. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_SCOPE_GRAPH">
      <Goal>Select which scopes exist, then place each by its relationships before its edges.</Goal>
      <Action>
        On greenfield init (graph still empty), propose scope candidates in ONE multiSelect
        `AskUserQuestion` call (`multiSelect: true`) derived from the just-locked Vision — never asked as
        free text first. Draw each candidate's boundary per `AX_SEPARATION_OF_CONCERNS` (data/domain ↔
        UI/presentation ↔ infrastructure; a shared core only once ≥2 consumers need it): the product-scope (name derived from Vision, kebab-case), then `infra-base`
        tagged «(Recommended)» with description «гейты: type-check/test/lint — обязательна перед
        scaffold/execute», then `interface` / `library` candidates only when the Vision actually implies
        them. The operator clicks checkboxes rather than composing scope names from scratch; the tool's
        own free-text slot already covers a custom answer, so do not duplicate it as an extra bullet.
        Checking a box only registers that scope in the Scopes table — deep design of any one scope
        (its own vision, architecture, requirements, modules) stays out of this step and happens in its
        own later `scope` session, per the Mission's own boundary.
        For each scope now in play (freshly checked here, or already present on a later update), the
        relationship question comes FIRST (`AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD`): who will depend
        on it, what it depends on, and whether it is cross-cutting (used by ≥2 scopes). Probe narrow
        framing — «авторизация только для web? а API / будущие клиенты?». The answer drives three things:
        the «scope vs module» test (a thing used inside one scope may be a module, not a scope →
        `H_SCOPE_VS_MODULE_AMBIGUITY`); the `depends-on` edges; and, when it is cross-cutting, a note that
        cross-scope scenarios or an interface scope may be needed. If the change REMOVES or RENAMES a
        scope, first surface what it orphans — its `specs/<scope>/…` spec, tasks, and inbound references
        — and resolve with the operator (move / delete / repoint); never silently leave orphans. Then
        propose scope-type with rationale — edge semantics (A→B = A depends-on B), acyclicity, and
        scope-type constraints per `AX_SCOPE_GRAPH_DISCIPLINE` (a cycle → `H_CYCLE_IN_GRAPH`). Show
        the graph to the operator as a layered ASCII diagram sourced from the `[GRAPH]` block
        `sdd-state` already put in context (levels + edges) — never re-derive levels by re-reading
        the Portal, and never print the Mermaid `graph TD` into the chat message: that fenced block
        is file content, it belongs only inside `specs/README.md` (mermaid belongs to the file,
        printing it in chat is a display violation). Layout: one horizontal tier per level, stacked
        top → bottom in DESCENDING level order (highest level / widest consumers on top, the
        level-0 foundation on the bottom row — what stands on the foundation is drawn resting
        above it). Every node name appears EXACTLY ONCE, in its own tier. Arrows point DOWN, from a
        dependent to its dependency, using only `│ ▼ ├ └ ─` (no other glyph). One parent fanning out
        to several children: draw a small tree under the parent, `├──▼──` for every child but the
        last, `└──▼──` for the last. Several parents converging on the same child (a shared
        dependency, e.g. the foundation scope): write the child ONCE, with a `▼` under each parent's
        column pointing at that single line. Example — input `[GRAPH]` (уровень 0: infra-base;
        уровень 1: api, worker; уровень 2: app; рёбра: app → api, worker · api → infra-base ·
        worker → infra-base) renders as:
        ```
                 app
                ▼   ▼
              api   worker
                ▼   ▼
              infra-base
        ```
        Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_INFRA_BOOTSTRAP">
      <Goal>Materialize the minimal infra-base spec if tooling was declared.</Goal>
      <Action>
        No tooling declared → skip. Else, per `AX_INFRA_BASE_BOOTSTRAP`,
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/infra-base-minimal-spec.xml")`,
        then scaffold the file via the CLI — `npx gennady sdd-new infrastructure --scope infra-base`
        (it creates directories itself and prints the section manifest; writing the spec from a blank
        Write instead of the scaffold is the same violation as hand-writing the portal skeleton) —
        and fill the skeleton to that format (scope-type infrastructure,
        one-sentence Vision, Tool Stack table, note pointing to `discovery infra-base` for the full
        Decision Log — the bootstrap only locks the tool choice, the full `infra` flow comes later).
        Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_PORTAL_WRITE">
      <Goal>Write / update `specs/README.md`.</Goal>
      <Action>
        When `specs/README.md` does not exist yet, create its skeleton via `sdd-new portal` first —
        it never overwrites and prints the section manifest (which sections are REQUIRED and what to
        fill); when it already exists, edit it in place, skip `sdd-new`. Content guidance per
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/portal-structure.xml")` and assemble the
        Portal to that format: the structured Vision block confirmed at STEP_1 (target-state paragraph
        plus Для кого / Боль / Успех / Принципы), and, per `AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED`, a Decision Log row
        for every deferred or release-boundary decision routed there in the same step — the section is
        omitted only when STEP_1 routed no such decision. Scope status (✅ / 🚧) comes from `sdd-state`
        (presence of `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md`), not a manual stat — rows per
        `AX_SCOPE_TABLE_FORMAT`.
        Reconcile any placeholder rows left by the scope flow into full rows — root is the primary
        owner of `specs/README.md` and the only flow that rewrites it (`AX_PORTAL_PRIMARY_OWNER`).
        Write the file. Then run `sdd-check --all .` to verify portal integrity —
        graph acyclicity, graph↔table↔spec coherence, no orphan specs or dangling scope references — and
        resolve any finding before finishing, per `AX_CLOSE_WITH_INTEGRITY_CHECK`. Approval Check.

        The flow never ends on bare text telling the operator to run `/sdd` themselves — close per
        `NEXT_STEP_MENU_FORMAT`: one ✅ line (portal written, integrity checked), then ONE
        `AskUserQuestion`. Option 1, recommended while any scope still lacks a spec: «Продолжить
        спеки: discovery `<следующий скоуп по графу зависимостей>`» — the first UNSPEC'D scope in
        Scope Graph dependency order (the level-0 / foundation scope, e.g. `infra-base` when
        present, else the lowest-level scope just added). On this pick, do NOT end the session —
        hand control to that scope's branch of the router's own `LOGIC_SWITCH` immediately (the
        `scope` or `infra` case, by that scope's scope-type), in the SAME session, exactly as
        `LOGIC_SWITCH`'s multi-scope case already chains branches. A scaffold option is offered
        instead ONLY once every scope in the graph already has a spec — per `NEXT_STEP_MENU_FORMAT`
        it is never recommended over an outstanding spec (specs need the operator's judgment,
        scaffold is mechanical decomposition of what is already approved). Last option always:
        «Закончить сессию» — on this pick, print a short resume instruction instead (`/sdd` + name
        the scope to design next) and stop; no directive loads. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <ChatOutput>
    Service line — dim, internal, never narrated (per `AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION`):
    `🗺️ ROOT_R<N> · <project> · <mode> · conf=<LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH>`. Breadcrumb milestones for this
    flow (per `BREADCRUMB_FORMAT`): Vision · Scope Graph · Infra · Portal.

